@@ -54,3 +54,8 @@ The owner reports the lab Dock was nonfunctional on the phone; the recording is
 
 ## Follow-up: owner still cannot see the mobile specimen
 The previous portal-based repair failed the owner's phone check. Remove it rather than mark it successful. The lab now renders one unconditional navigation specimen in normal document flow at every viewport. Route-scoped CSS overrides inherited fixed positioning and translation on the specimen only. On mobile it remains a five-column row; on desktop it retains the vertical layout. L42 is a visible lab marker to distinguish this revision. This deliberately tests the component inside the workbench, not the production Dock's viewport anchoring. The original cause is not established; fresh phone visibility/touch confirmation remains required.
+
+## L43 — reduce effects and consolidate motion ownership
+The owner confirmed L42 visibility and supplied phone/desktop failure recordings. Phone selected-state/lens mismatch is confirmed; the exact runtime scheduler failure is not isolated. The shared custom RAF spring wrapper is replaced by the already installed Motion engine, preserving its consumer API, release velocity and current position. Navigation also resets on blur/background if a pointer-up is lost. Chromium selection/retarget/vertical and horizontal drag were exercised; device acceptance remains open.
+
+Remove global pointer-following radial lighting. Vertical skew is zero rather than an angle amplified by a wide row; desktop specimen width is constrained to sidebar proportions. The new optional menu has one clipped material viewport with bounds driven by one spring and unscaled foreground. This accepts layout work for a small menu instead of transforming a filtered sibling with WebKit occlusion artifacts. It is not compositor-only and no performance improvement is claimed. Chromium menu selection, Escape and interrupted closing/reopening succeeded. Actual iPhone fallback rendering and new gesture behavior require a new device recording.
